    <body>
      <sec id="article-25074.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Methicillin-resistant <italic toggle="yes">Staphylococcus aureus</italic> (MRSA) emerged shortly after the introduction of methicillin in 1959, transforming from an isolated laboratory finding into a global health challenge. Defined by the presence of the <italic toggle="yes">mecA</italic> or <italic toggle="yes">mecC</italic> gene within the staphylococcal cassette chromosome mec (SCCmec), MRSA demonstrates resistance to beta-lactam antibiotics through altered penicillin-binding proteins that maintain cell wall synthesis despite antimicrobial pressure. This course reviews the evolution of MRSA into hospital- and community-associated forms and their differing clinical features (ranging from mild skin lesions to life-threatening conditions), molecular biology, antibiotic susceptibility, and treatment. The emergence of vancomycin-intermediate and -resistant strains and the urgency of continued surveillance and therapeutic innovation are also discussed.</p>
        <p>This activity explores MRSA&#x02019;s molecular mechanisms, virulence factors, diagnostic methods, and evolving resistance patterns. Participants will strengthen their ability to select appropriate empiric and targeted therapies, interpret laboratory data, and implement evidence-based infection control strategies.&#x000a0;This activity for healthcare professionals is designed to enhance the learner's competence in recognizing MRSA, performing the recommended evaluation, optimizing antimicrobial stewardship, and implementing an appropriate interprofessional approach when managing this condition.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Identify molecular mechanisms responsible for methicillin resistance in <italic toggle="yes">Staphylococcus aureus&#x000a0;</italic>infections.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Differentiate between hospital-associated and community-associated methicillin-resistant&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Staphylococcus aureus</italic>&#x000a0;based on clinical features.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Interpret laboratory data to confirm methicillin-resistant&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Staphylococcus aureus</italic> infection.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Collaborate with interprofessional teams to implement infection prevention measures to reduce methicillin-resistant&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Staphylococcus aureus</italic> transmission in healthcare settings.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=25074&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=25074">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25074.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Methicillin-resistant&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Staphylococcus aureus</italic>&#x000a0;(MRSA) infection is one of the leading causes of hospital-acquired infections and is commonly associated with significant morbidity, mortality, increased length of stay, and a high cost burden.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r1">[1]</xref>&#x000a0;Soon after methicillin entered clinical use in 1959, Professor Patricia Jevons identified methicillin resistance in <italic toggle="yes">Staphylococcus aureus</italic> (S. aureus) in 1960. In February 1961, <italic toggle="yes">The British Medical Journal</italic> published her observation of this resistant strain, now recognized as MRSA. The pathogen rapidly established endemicity in hospitals across the United Kingdom and, over time, expanded into a widespread global health concern.</p>
        <p>MRSA is defined by the presence of the 20 to 65 kb staphylococcal cassette chromosome mec (SCC<italic toggle="yes">mec)</italic>&#x000a0;element inserted within the&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">orfX</italic>&#x000a0;(an RNA methyltransferase) gene of&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">S aureus</italic>. SCC<italic toggle="yes">mec</italic>&#x000a0;contains the&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">mecA</italic>&#x000a0;gene complex (responsible for methicillin resistance) and a set of site-specific recombinase genes (<italic toggle="yes">ccrA</italic>&#x000a0;and&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">ccrB</italic>) responsible for its mobility.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r2">[2]</xref>&#x000a0;Oxacillin is a derivative of methicillin and is used to test for MRSA. Because of methicillin's historic role, we still use the acronym MRSA to describe these isolates.</p>
        <p>MRSA&#x000a0;carries the&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">mecA</italic>&#x000a0;and&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">mecC</italic>&#x000a0;genes, which confer oxacillin resistance by&#x000a0;encoding a different penicillin-binding protein (PBP) that allows cell wall synthesis in the presence of beta-lactams due to low affinity for binding.&#x000a0;According to the Clinical and Laboratory Standards Institute (CLSI), MRSA is defined as&#x000a0;isolates with a methicillin minimum inhibitory concentration (MIC) of 4 &#x003bc;g/mL or greater or an oxacillin MIC of greater than 2 &#x003bc;g/mL.&#x000a0;The <italic toggle="yes">mecA</italic> gene was first identified in Japan in 1987. The discovery of <italic toggle="yes">mecC</italic> in 2011 (in England) was significant because it represented a new mechanism of methicillin resistance in MRSA, distinct from the more common <italic toggle="yes">mecA</italic> gene.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>MRSA infections can be further divided into hospital-associated (HA-MRSA) and community-associated (CA-MRSA) infections, which differ not only in their clinical features and molecular biology but also in their antibiotic susceptibility and treatment.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;Consequently, MRSA represents a significant challenge in modern infectious disease management. Furthermore,&#x000a0;the ongoing emergence of vancomycin-intermediate and -resistant strains further underscores the need for vigilant diagnosis, antimicrobial stewardship, and evidence-based therapeutic strategies to mitigate resistance and improve outcomes.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25074.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>MRSA are strains of <italic toggle="yes">S aureus</italic> that have developed resistance to beta-lactam and many other antibiotics. MRSA strains are transmitted by direct skin-to-skin contact as well as indirect contact of surfaces. MRSA is not inherently more virulent than methicillin-sensitive <italic toggle="yes">S aureus</italic>. Please see StatPearls' companion resource, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/29453"><italic toggle="yes">Staphylococcus aureus</italic>&#x000a0;Infections</ext-link>," for further information.&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25074.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>The history of MRSA infection dates back to 1961, when the organism was first described. Since that time, the incidence and prevalence of MRSA infections have risen dramatically. Recent population-based studies have suggested a reduction in hospital-acquired MRSA (HA-MRSA) incidence within the United States, although this improvement&#x000a0;was at the expense of a growing prevalence of community-acquired MRSA (CA-MRSA). Reported MRSA infection rates range from 7% to 60%.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r5">[5]</xref> Until the 1990s, MRSA primarily affected individuals with healthcare exposure, but subsequent cases began appearing among people with no prior hospitalization, leading to the classification of MRSA into hospital-acquired and community-acquired forms. Globally, MRSA incidence rose sharply throughout the 1990s and peaked around 2005. In the United States, HA-MRSA rates declined by 17.1% from 2005 to 2012,<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r6">[6]</xref> likely due to improved adherence to infection control measures. The rate of decline slowed from 2013 to 2016, while CA-MRSA showed a modest increase between 2012 and 2017.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r7">[7]</xref></p>
        <p>
<italic toggle="yes">Staphylococcus aureus</italic> commonly colonizes the nares, followed by the oropharynx, skin, and gastrointestinal tract. In intensive care settings, the rectum and throat serve as the primary sites of MRSA colonization. Among carriers, nasal strains most frequently cause subsequent infection, and nasal carriers face a 5-fold greater risk of healthcare-associated infection than noncarriers.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r8">[8]</xref> Nasal colonization represents a&#x000a0;major risk factor for later infection. In the United States, MRSA colonization rates range between 0.9% and 1.5%.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r9">[9]</xref> Transmission of <italic toggle="yes">S aureus</italic>, including MRSA, occurs through direct person-to-person contact or indirectly via contaminated fomites, eg, hospital surfaces, household objects, clothing, athletic gear, and personal hygiene items.&#x000a0;Please see StatPearls' companion resource, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/29453"><italic toggle="yes">Staphylococcus aureus</italic>&#x000a0;Infections</ext-link>," for further information.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Individuals&#x000a0;at increased risk of MRSA infection include the following:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Individuals&#x000a0;who inject drugs</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Individuals with frequent healthcare contact</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Individuals in congregant living facilities</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Prisoners</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Military personnel</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Athletes who share gym equipment</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Children</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Older adults</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Individuals with HIV</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Individuals in urban and underserved environments</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Since the 2000s, the genotypic differences between HA-MRSA and CA-MRSA have begun to homogenize.&#x000a0;MRSA isolates exhibit more extraordinary genetic diversity globally. The most common variant in the&#x000a0;United States is strain type ST8, whereas ST5 is the most common in South America, and ST22 and ST30 are common in Europe.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r10">[10]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25074.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>
<bold>Antibiotic Resistance</bold>
</p>
        <p>The&#x000a0;primary reason for MRSA resistance to semisynthetic antistaphylococcal penicillins is due to the presence of the <italic toggle="yes">mecA</italic> gene sequence, which is known to generate transpeptidase PB2a, lowering the organism's affinity to bind to beta-lactam antibiotics.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;The <italic toggle="yes">mecA</italic> gene sequence&#x000a0;is located on a mobile chromosomal element termed <italic toggle="yes">SCCmec</italic>.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r3">[3]</xref></p>
        <p>Additionally, MRSA isolates with intermediate and high-level glycopeptide resistance have been identified over the past 25 years and are associated with treatment failure with vancomycin and daptomycin.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r11">[11]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r13">[13]</xref>&#x000a0;Their global prevalence currently remains low, varying between geographic regions. Furthermore, the intermediate strains can initially appear susceptible using routine laboratory methods.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r14">[14]</xref> The discovery&#x000a0; of these strains led to the following modification in previously established vancomycin susceptibility breakpoints:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Susceptible MIC&#x000a0;&#x0003c;2 &#x000b5;g/mL&#x000a0;<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.eucast.org/clinical_breakpoints">[EUCAST.&#x000a0;Clinical Breakpoints: Breakpoints and Guidance. January 2019]</ext-link></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Intermediate-resistant MIC 4 to 8 &#x000b5;g/mL</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>High-level-resistant MIC&#x000a0;&#x0003e;16 &#x000b5;g/mL&#x000a0;<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://clsi.org/shop/standards/m07/">[CLSI. Methods for Dilution Antimicrobial Susceptibility Tests for Bacteria that Grow Aerobically. March 19, 2024]</ext-link>.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>The European Committee on Antimicrobial Susceptibility Testing (EUCAST) classifies <italic toggle="yes">S aureus</italic> strains with vancomycin MICs greater than 2 &#x000b5;g/mL as resistant.<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.eucast.org/clinical_breakpoints">[EUCAST.&#x000a0;Clinical Breakpoints: Breakpoints and Guidance. January 2019]</ext-link>&#x000a0;Clinical isolates with phenotypes identified as having a vancomycin MIC of &#x0003c;2&#x000b5;g/mL exist that contain subpopulations with higher MICs. These clinical isolates have been labeled "heterogeneous and heteroresistant."<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r13">[13]</xref></p>
        <p>The&#x000a0;term&#x000a0;vancomycin-intermediate <italic toggle="yes">S aureus </italic>(VISA)<italic toggle="yes">&#x000a0;</italic>is confusing for several reasons. Initially,&#x000a0;VISA can appear susceptible when tested with routine laboratory methods. However, clinical isolates with phenotypes identified as having a vancomycin MIC of &#x0003c;2 &#x000b5;g/mL contain subpopulations with higher MICs.&#x000a0;These subpopulations are capable of growing in the presence of various concentrations of vancomycin (eg, &#x0003c;2 &#x000b5;g/mL to 8 &#x000b5;g/mL) and are termed heterogeneous or heteroresistant vancomycin-intermediate&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">S aureus</italic>&#x000a0;(hVISA).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r13">[13]</xref></p>
        <p>The CLSI designates the intermediate breakpoint range for VISA as 4 to 8 &#x000b5;g/mL. However, the EUCAST does not&#x000a0;use an intermediate&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">S aureus</italic> classification; instead, strains with breakpoints greater than 2 &#x000b5;g/mL are considered resistant in their classification scheme. Both hVISA and VISA strains&#x000a0;emerge from multiple mutations driven by prolonged vancomycin treatment.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r15">[15]</xref>&#x000a0;Many authors believe that hVISA is a precursor of VISA. Disc diffusion methods are unreliable at identifying hVISA and VISA, and agar or broth dilution methods are recommended on that basis.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>High-level <italic toggle="yes">S aureus</italic> vancomycin resistance occurs by plasmid-mediated transfer of resistance genes from vancomycin-resistant <italic toggle="yes">Enterococcus faecalis</italic>.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r16">[16]</xref>&#x000a0;These vancomycin-resistant&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">S&#x000a0;</italic><italic toggle="yes">aureus</italic>&#x000a0;(VRSA) strains can grow at vancomycin concentrations exceeding 32 &#x000b5;g/mL.&#x000a0;(Please refer to&#x000a0;the&#x000a0;<bold>Treatment</bold>&#x000a0;section for more information on antibiotic resistance and therapeutic options.)</p>
        <p>
<bold>Virulence Factors</bold>
</p>
        <p>Virulence factors of MRSA are mediated by its cell wall structures, toxins, and enzymes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r17">[17]</xref>&#x000a0;Please see StatPearls' companion resource, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/29453"><italic toggle="yes">Staphylococcus aureus</italic>&#x000a0;Infections</ext-link>," for&#x000a0;additional information.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Cell wall structures</bold>
</p>
        <p>Capsular polysaccharides enhance the virulence of <italic toggle="yes">S aureus</italic>&#x000a0;by impairing antibody-mediated opsonization, complement activation, and phagocytosis.&#x000a0;Staphylococcal protein A (surface-associated protein) is a cell wall structure that protects the microorganism from phagocytosis by attaching to the circulating IgG.&#x000a0;Cell-surface clumping factor molecules initiate&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">S</italic><italic toggle="yes">&#x000a0;aureus</italic>-fibrinogen adherence.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Extracellular toxins</bold>
</p>
        <p>MRSA expresses alpha, beta, gamma, and delta (hemolysin) toxins. Among these toxins, alpha toxin is considered a major virulence factor.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r18">[18]</xref>&#x000a0;Staphylococcal enterotoxins A, B, C, D, and E are the main virulence factors in human food poisoning; these are pyrogenic exotoxins belonging to <italic toggle="yes">S aureus</italic> superantigens. These bacterial enterotoxins stimulate IL-4 and IL-10 expression, leading to the activation of TH2 cells and subsequently suppressing the clearance of invading microorganisms.</p>
        <p>Panton-Valentine leucocidin (PVL) is a potent exotoxin produced by MRSA.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r19">[19]</xref>&#x000a0;PVL targets and destroys the plasma membranes of polymorphonuclear cells. Additionally, PVL stimulates these cells to release reactive oxygen species, interleukin 8, and lysozymes, as well as triggering histamine release from basophils.&#x000a0;Toxic shock syndrome toxin (TSST) is one of the most potent antigens produced by <italic toggle="yes">S aureus</italic> and is implicated in toxic shock syndrome in humans. This disease occurs when TSST is released, triggering the production of tumor necrosis factor-alpha (TNF-&#x003b1;), interleukin 1 (IL-1), and interleukin 2 (IL-2).&#x000a0;Staphylococcal exfoliative toxins are serine proteases that cause staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome and bullous impetigo. Staphylococcal exfoliative toxins destroy the desmoglein-1 protein, causing damage to the epidermis.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Extracellular enzymes</bold>
</p>
        <p>MRSA causes plasma coagulation by releasing the coagulase (Coa) enzyme. Additionally, the Coa enzyme contains a D1D2 domain that specifically binds prothrombin. Staphylokinase is an extracellular enzyme that activates plasminogen, leading to fibrin clot lysis and subsequent bacterial propagation. Consequently, MRSA strains that produce staphylokinase typically exhibit reduced biofilm development in both in vitro and noninvasive human infections. Staphylococcal nucleases are heat-stable, calcium-dependent enzymes that destroy DNA and RNA substrates. Staphylococcal proteases help bacteria disseminate by evading the host defense. <italic toggle="yes">Staphylococcus aureus</italic> hyaluronidase facilitates bacterial migration by degrading hyaluronic acid in the extracellular matrix and biofilms.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r20">[20]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25074.s6" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>MRSA can cause a range of organ-specific infections, the most common being the skin and subcutaneous tissues, followed by invasive infections like bacteremia, osteomyelitis, meningitis, pneumonia, lung abscess, and empyema. Infective endocarditis caused by MRSA is associated with increased morbidity and mortality compared to any other organism. People who inject drugs are at particularly high risk for MRSA endocarditis.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Additionally, MRSA is capable of infecting every organ. MRSA can also cause a variety of toxin-mediated infections.&#x000a0;Please see StatPearls' companion resources, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/18595">Breast Abscess</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/26978">Perinephric Abscess</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/166078">Renal Abscess</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/30108">Septic Thrombophlebitis</ext-link>," and "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/30298">Toxic Shock Syndrome</ext-link>" for additional details.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Skin and Soft Tissue Infections</bold>
</p>
        <p>CA-MRSA is a predominant organism associated with&#x000a0;skin and soft tissue infections (SSTIs), eg, cellulitis, necrotizing fasciitis, and diabetic foot ulcers. CA-MRSA is also increasingly associated with more invasive diseases than those not due to MRSA. More frequently, these infections are multidrug-resistant, leading to frequent recurrence, increased hospitalization, and mortality.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r6">[6]</xref>&#x000a0;CA-MRSA is a common cause of skin lesions that are often incorrectly attributed to spider bites.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r21">[21]</xref>&#x000a0;Please see StatPearls' companion resources, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/19114">Cellulitis</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/23410">Impetigo</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/21289">Erysipelas</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/21289">Folliculitis</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/18860">Carbuncles</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/25623">Necrotizing Fasciitis</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/29451">Staphylococcal Scalded Skin Syndrome</ext-link>," and "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/18595">Breast Abscess</ext-link>" for&#x000a0;additional information.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Bone and joint infection</bold>
</p>
        <p>Staphylococci are the most common cause of bone and joint infections. MRSA can cause osteomyelitis of the spine and long bones of the upper and lower extremities by extension of local infection from a wound or as a part of a hematogenous infection. Similarly,&#x000a0;MRSA can cause septic arthritis&#x000a0;of both native and prosthetic joints.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r22">[22]</xref>&#x000a0;Please see StatPearls' companion resources, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/17857">Septic Arthritis</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/28907">Septic Arthritis of the Pediatric Hip</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/28905">Septic Bursitis</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/26397">Osteomyelitis</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/26400">Vertebral Osteomyelitis</ext-link>," and "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/27864">Periprosthetic Joint Infections</ext-link>," for additional details.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Pneumonia</bold>
</p>
        <p>Staphylococcal pneumonia, historically known as postinfluenza pneumonia, was a distinct clinical entity with a dramatic onset of respiratory symptoms and mortality ranging from 80% to 90% in the preantibiotic era. The condition carried specific radiological features, including cavitary lesions, empyema, and pyopneumothorax, as well as pathological features,&#x000a0;eg, pulmonary hemorrhage and microabscess formation.</p>
        <p>In the postantibiotic period, the course has been less explosive, not always associated with viral influenza, related to other risk factors for <italic toggle="yes">S aureus</italic> infections, and carries a mortality of around 30% to 40%. Nevertheless, CA-MRSA, causing life-threatening necrotizing pneumonia in otherwise healthy individuals, has been reported across the United States recently.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r23">[23]</xref> Necrotizing pneumonia&#x000a0;is characterized by severe respiratory symptoms, high fevers, hemoptysis, and hypotension, and rapidly progresses to sepsis and septic shock with leukopenia and elevated C-reactive protein. Multilobar cavitating alveolar infiltrates in this clinical setting are consistent with CA-MRSA infection.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>MRSA is also a leading cause of hospital-acquired and ventilator-associated pneumonia. Hospital-acquired pneumonia, or nosocomial pneumonia,&#x000a0;is characterized as pneumonia developing 48 hours or more after hospital admission, indicating that this condition was not incubating at the time of admission. Ventilator-associated pneumonia (VAP) is defined as pneumonia developing&#x000a0;48 hours or more after implementation of endotracheal intubation and mechanical ventilation, and was not present before intubation. The microbiological etiology of these conditions is similar and carries a grave prognosis associated with poor overall outcomes. Please see StatPearls' companion resource, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/29450">Staphylococcal Pneumonia</ext-link>,"&#x000a0;for additional details.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Bacteremia</bold>
</p>
        <p>Bacteremia due to&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">S aureus</italic>&#x000a0;has been reported to be associated with mortality rates of 15% to 60%. Typical of bloodstream infections due to methicillin-sensitive <italic toggle="yes">S aureus</italic> (MSSA), those due to MRSA are often associated with complicated metastatic and endovascular infections.&#x000a0;Endocarditis should be ruled out in any patient with MRSA bacteremia. Please see StatPearls' companion resource, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/29453"><italic toggle="yes">Staphylococcus aureus</italic>&#x000a0;Infections</ext-link>," for&#x000a0;additional information.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Endocarditis</bold>
</p>
        <p>MRSA is a significant cause of bacterial endocarditis, which carries a mortality rate of between 30% and 37%. Right-sided MRSA endocarditis is commonly associated with intravenous drug use and intravenous catheters. Patients with tricuspid valve vegetations may have septic pulmonary emboli, which cause nodular infiltrates and cavitating lesions in the lungs. Similarly, patients with involvement of the mitral and aortic valves may have secondary infections in distant foci,&#x000a0;eg, bones and joints, the kidneys, the brain, and other organs. Obtaining a history and thoroughly examining these patients is important, combined with the necessary&#x000a0;laboratory studies and&#x000a0;radiological tests.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r24">[24]</xref>&#x000a0;Please see StatPearls' companion resource, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/21086">Endocarditis</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/21087">Endocarditis Antibiotic Regimens</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/27867">Prosthetic Valve Endocarditis</ext-link>," and "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/18876">Cardiac Abscess</ext-link>" for additional details.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Meningitis</bold>
</p>
        <p>Meningitis is defined by isolation of MRSA in 1 or more cerebral spinal fluid (CSF) or blood cultures, with signs and symptoms of acute meningitis and typical CSF findings, eg, pleocytosis (&#x0003e;10 cells/&#x003bc;L), decreased glucose level (&#x0003c;0.40 mg/dL), or increased protein concentration (&#x0003e;0.45 mg/dL). Meningitis can result from metastatic bloodstream infection or from direct extension from a postoperative wound. Furthermore, MRSA meningitis is associated with high mortality (10%-45%).&#x000a0;This condition usually presents with altered mental status, rapid onset of fever, meningeal signs, and headache. Rarely do patients present with focal neurological deficits and seizures.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r25">[25]</xref>&#x000a0;Please see StatPearls' companion resources, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/21222">Epidural Abscess</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/26805">Brain Abscess</ext-link>," and "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/24965">Bacterial Meningitis</ext-link>" for further information.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25074.s7" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>When evaluating patients with presumptive or proven MRSA infection,&#x000a0;clinicians should consider the following general questions:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Does the patient have risk factors for MRSA?&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>In the absence of risk factors, are there features of the infection that suggest MRSA may be the etiology?</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Is the infection limited to a single anatomic site, or has it disseminated to other sites?</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Does the patient have co-morbidities or socioeconomic&#x000a0;factors that will impact the choice of antibiotic treatment?</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>What are the potential benefits and risks of the chosen antibiotic and possible surgical option?</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Once the current MRSA infection has been successfully treated, what strategies can be used to reduce the risk of recurrent MRSA infections?</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Clinical suspicion in patients with risk factors for MRSA infection is crucial for diagnostic and therapeutic interventions. However, confirmation of MRSA infection should not delay treatment with empiric MRSA antibiotics. Clinicians should&#x000a0;submit samples from suspected sources of infection for analysis, including blood, sputum, urine, wounds, CSF, and surgical specimens.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r26">[26]</xref>&#x000a0;A Gram stain demonstrating the presence of Gram-positive cocci in clusters is suggestive of <italic toggle="yes">S aureus.</italic>&#x000a0;Polymerase&#x000a0;chain reaction (PCR) for MRSA identification is a valuable, rapid test while awaiting culture confirmation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r10">[10]</xref></p>
        <p>PCR identification of MRSA from the nares is a frequently employed&#x000a0;screening test to rule out MRSA colonization.&#x000a0;PCR is not a confirmatory test for MRSA infection, but a negative test has high specificity in ruling out MRSA infection.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r22">[22]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25074.s8" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>The selection of&#x000a0;empirical antibiotic therapy for treating MRSA infections depends on the type of disease, local&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">S aureus</italic>&#x000a0;resistance patterns, drug availability, adverse-effect&#x000a0;profile, and individual patient characteristics.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r27">[27]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r28">[28]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r29">[29]</xref>&#x000a0;Clinicians should recognize that guidelines on the type and duration of antibiotic therapy are often based on limited data; thus, treatment should be tailored on a case-by-case basis. Please see StatPearls' companion resource, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/29453"><italic toggle="yes">Staphylococcus aureus</italic>&#x000a0;Infections</ext-link>," for&#x000a0;additional information.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>A vancomycin trough level between 15 and 20 mg/L&#x000a0;may result in an area under the curve to minimum inhibitory concentration (AUC/MIC) ratio greater than necessary for clinical efficacy. A vancomycin AUC/MIC of 400 to 600 mg&#x022c5;h/L is associated with better clinical outcomes in the treatment of&#x000a0;MRSA infections.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r30">[30]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Skin and Soft Tissue Infections</bold>
</p>
        <p>Superficial infections,&#x000a0;eg, impetigo, are treated with gentle soap and water cleansing followed by topical mupirocin. For most uncomplicated SSTIs,&#x000a0;empirical treatment is with oral antibiotics, eg, trimethoprim/sulfamethoxazole, tetracyclines (eg, doxycycline or minocycline), or clindamycin. Higher doses&#x000a0;of trimethoprim/sulfamethoxazole 160/800 mg,&#x000a0;1 tablet&#x000a0;3 times daily or&#x000a0;2 tablets&#x000a0;twice daily in adults are recommended for MRSA in patients with normal renal function. Newer agents,&#x000a0;eg, linezolid, tedizolid, and delafloxacin, can also be used as&#x000a0;alternative oral regimens if available and deemed cost-effective.</p>
        <p>Parenteral antibiotics are indicated for invasive SSTIs or with signs of systemic involvement, inadequate response to oral therapy, or if an SSTI occurs adjacent to an indwelling device.</p>
        <p>Intravenous vancomycin is the drug of choice for most MRSA infections in hospitalized patients. This agent can be used as empiric and definitive therapy, as&#x000a0;most MRSA infections are susceptible to vancomycin. As noted above, some sporadic cases of hVISA, VISA, and VRSA infections may not respond to glycopeptide antibiotics. The dosage depends on the type and severity of the infection. Vancomycin trough is obtained just before the fourth dose to ascertain a therapeutic level. The goal trough range is typically between 10 and 20 &#x000b5;g/mL. For complicated infections, the goal is 15 to 20 &#x000b5;g/mL. The dose should be adjusted based on serum trough levels or AUC/MIC and according to renal function.</p>
        <p>Daptomycin is a suitable parenteral alternative when vancomycin is unavailable or not tolerated. Other short-acting options include ceftaroline and telavancin. Long-acting treatment options include dalbavancin and oritavancin. Regardless of the initial empiric antibiotic choice, subsequent therapy should be tailored based on the careful review of culture and susceptibility data.</p>
        <p>The duration of therapy for MRSA SSTIs typically ranges from 5 to 14 days, depending on the extent of the infection and the response to treatment.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Bacteremia</bold>
</p>
        <p>Source control is a crucial component of MRSA bacteremia treatment, in conjunction with empiric MRSA coverage until susceptibility results are available.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r31">[31]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r32">[32]</xref>&#x000a0;Follow-up blood cultures, echocardiography, and consultation by an Infectious Diseases physician are recommended quality-of-care indicators.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r33">[33]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r34">[34]</xref> Please see StatPearls' companion resource, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/29453"><italic toggle="yes">Staphylococcus aureus</italic>&#x000a0;Infections</ext-link>," for further details.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Vancomycin and daptomycin are considered the standard of care according to the 2011 Infectious Diseases Society of America guidelines.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r35">[35]</xref>&#x000a0;However, since the establishment of the guidelines, mortality due to MRSA bacteremia has not significantly changed, and remains between 20% to 30%.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r36">[36]</xref>&#x000a0;Efforts to improve therapeutic response have led to the investigation of combination glycopeptide plus beta-lactam treatment in patients with MRSA bacteremia.&#x000a0;Vancomycin and daptomycin, combined with a variety of beta-lactams, have generally demonstrated a shorter duration of bacteremia&#x000a0;than the glycopeptide alone. Impact on mortality has met with mixed results; some studies demonstrate a reduction, others demonstrate no effect, and still others raise concern for increased risk due to the development of acute kidney injury in the patients receiving the antibiotic combination.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r37">[37]</xref>&#x000a0;While additional studies are needed to establish safety, efficacy, and reduction in mortality, the use of combination antibiotic therapy should be considered for high-risk patients and those with refractory bacteremia. When to initiate combination therapy and for how long remains to be determined. However, it has been suggested that induction with combination antibiotics, followed by streamlining to monotherapy, makes biological sense.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r37">[37]</xref>&#x000a0;Which combinations and which patient population to target are areas of unresolved but great interest.</p>
        <p>Teicoplanin is a bacteriostatic glycopeptide with a similar spectrum of activity and efficacy as vancomycin and is better tolerated than vancomycin.&#x000a0;However,&#x000a0;teicoplanin is used less commonly due to its limited availability. Teicoplanin&#x000a0;is not FDA-approved and is not available in the United States. The use of rifampin, gentamicin, linezolid,&#x000a0;quinupristin-dalfopristin, tigecycline, or fluoroquinolones has no role in the treatment of&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">S aureus</italic>&#x000a0;bacteremia.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Follow-up cultures should be repeated to document clearance of the infection from the bloodstream. Persistent, positive cultures after 48 hours of treatment should prompt further evaluation related to drug susceptibility and source control.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Endocarditis</bold>
</p>
        <p>The 2015 American Heart Association (AHA) guidelines recommend intravenous vancomycin as the first-line treatment for endocarditis. For patients who can neither tolerate vancomycin nor achieve adequate dosing, intravenous daptomycin is an alternative option. The recommended duration of therapy for native valve endocarditis is&#x000a0;6 weeks. Prosthetic valve endocarditis secondary to MRSA should&#x000a0;be treated with intravenous vancomycin and rifampin for 6 weeks, and gentamicin should&#x000a0;be given in the first 2 weeks of therapy. However, evidence of additional benefit of combining rifampin or gentamicin with vancomycin or daptomycin for native valve endocarditis is absent. Combination glycopeptide plus beta-lactams, telavancin, ceftaroline, and quinupristin-dalfopristin&#x000a0;have been successfully used as salvage therapy in select patient groups.&#x000a0;Clindamycin or linezolid should not be used to treat endocarditis because they have poorer outcomes than cell-wall-active agents, eg, vancomycin and daptomycin.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r24">[24]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Pneumonia</bold>
</p>
        <p>For MRSA pneumonia, the recommended antibiotics are vancomycin (IV), linezolid (oral/IV at a dosage of 600 mg twice daily), or clindamycin 600 mg&#x000a0;3 times&#x000a0;daily if the strain is susceptible. Avoiding daptomycin is crucial because this agent is inactivated by surfactant.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r38">[38]</xref> Additionally, tigecycline is associated with an increased risk of mortality and should be used only as a last resort.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r39">[39]</xref>&#x000a0;Ceftaroline and ceftobiprole have been used off-label in the treatment of pneumonia due to MRSA.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r40">[40]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r41">[41]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Bone and Joint Infections</bold>
</p>
        <p>For osteomyelitis, intravenous vancomycin or daptomycin is recommended. Ceftaroline has been used off-label.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r42">[42]</xref> Oral options include&#x000a0;trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole in combination with rifampin, linezolid, tedizolid, or clindamycin. Oral linezolid and tedizolid are attractive agents due to their excellent bone penetration.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r43">[43]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r44">[44]</xref></p>
        <p>Early-onset prosthetic joint infections (less than 3 weeks) are treated with intravenous vancomycin or daptomycin, combined with rifampin, along with debridement while attempting to retain the device. In cases of late-onset infections or unstable implants, debridement with device removal is recommended.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r38">[38]</xref>&#x000a0;For spinal hardware infections in which the hardware is retained, a treatment duration of more than 12 weeks may be required.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r45">[45]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>CNS Infections</bold>
</p>
        <p>Meningitis is primarily treated with vancomycin. Alternative antibiotics that may be considered include linezolid, ceftaroline, and trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole.&#x000a0;Clinicians should note that daptomycin has poor penetration into the cerebrospinal fluid (CSF); therefore, if no other treatment options are available, it can be used in combination with rifampin.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r46">[46]</xref>&#x000a0;Limited data currently exist regarding the treatment of MRSA&#x000a0;intracranial abscess. Case reports suggest that&#x000a0;a combination of surgical drainage and either vancomycin or linezolid should be used.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r47">[47]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Salvage Therapy</bold>
</p>
        <p>Clinicians&#x000a0;will encounter complicated MRSA infections that, for reasons of resistance or patient intolerance, are not amenable to treatment with standard-of-care antibiotics.&#x000a0;It is unlikely&#x000a0;that robust&#x000a0;randomized clinical trials capable of evaluating the efficacy and safety of all possible antibiotic combinations that could be used to treat patients failing current therapy will be conducted. A myriad of case reports and case series that have utilized various antibiotic combinations to treat complicated MRSA infections have been published, and most report varying degrees of success. However, one must be cautious in drawing inferences due to the possibility of publication bias (ie, cases with negative outcomes often go unreported). Notwithstanding, salvage therapy using antibiotic combinations should be considered. These novel treatment approaches should be based on sound microbiological and pharmacological principles in the context of the specific MRSA strain resistance pattern and the infected host's characteristics.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r37">[37]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r48">[48]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r49">[49]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r50">[50]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r51">[51]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r52">[52]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25074.s9" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>Since <italic toggle="yes">S aureus</italic>, including MRSA, can infect every tissue and organ, cause indolent and life-threatening diseases, the differential diagnosis is extensive, and a complete list is beyond the scope of this discussion. Please see StatPearls' companion resource, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/29453"><italic toggle="yes">Staphylococcus aureus</italic>&#x000a0;Infections</ext-link>," for further information.&#x000a0;Some of the more commonly encountered diseases that should be included in the differential diagnosis include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Infectious or inflammatory skin-soft tissue disorders</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Sepsis syndrome and septic shock</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Abscess</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Endocarditis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Acute gastroenteritis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Wound infection</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Necrotizing fasciitis, most importantly, beta-hemolytic streptococci</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Acute arthritis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Acute bursitis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Spondylitis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Implanted medical device and prosthesis infection</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Pneumonia</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Meningitis</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>A frequently encountered situation in clinical practice is skin infections caused by CA-MRSA&#x000a0;being misidentified as spider bites.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r21">[21]</xref>&#x000a0;The patient presents with a painful, erythematosus pustule, occasionally associated with fever, and attributes the skin lesion to a bite by an unseen spider. Whereas biting spiders are exceedingly rare worldwide and almost vanishingly rare in North America, MRSA skin infections are&#x000a0;exceedingly common.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r53">[53]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25074.s10" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>The prognosis of MRSA infection depends on the anatomical sites involved, the patient's general health, source control, and the prompt administration of active antibiotics at therapeutic doses. Superficial skin infections are usually easily managed. Deep tissue infections can lead to fasciitis and require debridement in addition to antibiotics. Endovascular infections, including endocarditis, are always complicated and potentially life-threatening. Infections involving implanted medical devices and prostheses are complicated, often require removal of the implant, and prolonged courses of antibiotics. Mortality due to&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">S&#x000a0;</italic><italic toggle="yes">aureus&#x000a0;</italic>bacteremia&#x000a0;is in the range of 15% to 30%. The risk of death from an MRSA infection is highest among nursing home residents, older adults, individuals with liver or kidney disease, immunocompromised hosts, and patients with severe sepsis. Adherence to quality-of-care indicators&#x000a0;for patients with MRSA bacteremia is most important to reduce mortality.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r34">[34]</xref></p>
        <p>When determining treatment strategies, consideration of how to interpret and use guidelines in the context of the patient's unique risk factors is crucial. Management and treatment decisions should be implemented on&#x000a0;an individual basis. Consultation with an infectious disease specialist is advised for high-risk patients.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r54">[54]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25074.s11" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>Complications associated with MRSA infection include necrotizing fasciitis, abscess, venous thrombosis, bacteremia, endocarditis, bone and joint infections, pneumonia, sepsis, and septic shock.&#x000a0;The presence of MRSA infections is linked to several adverse outcomes, including longer stays in the ICU and hospital, as well as increased morbidity, mortality, and healthcare costs.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>The widespread use of vancomycin has resulted in the emergence of hVISA, VISA, and VRSA strains. When the clinical response is not optimal, alternative therapies should be considered.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25074.r55">[55]</xref>&#x000a0;Myriad potential complications can arise from adverse events associated with the antibiotics used to treat MRSA infections. A discussion of all of the potential antibiotic-associated adverse events is beyond the scope of this discussion.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25074.s12" sec-type="Consultations">
        <title>Consultations</title>
        <p>Infectious disease specialists should be consulted when managing&#x000a0;complicated MRSA infections,&#x000a0;eg, bacteremia, endocarditis, and bone and joint infections. For MRSA with reduced vancomycin susceptibility (VISA or VRSA), in addition to obtaining an infectious disease consultation, hospital infection control should also be notified.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25074.s13" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>Patients should be educated about the ease of MRSA transmission through contact and strategies to prevent colonization and infection. In particular, adherence to hand hygiene practices, avoidance of sharing personal hygiene items, and environmental cleaning should be stressed. People who inject drugs are at very high risk of acquiring complicated MRSA infections and should receive treatment for opioid use disorder. Education efforts should address the misconception that spider bites cause what is, in reality, likely to be an MRSA skin infection. Infection control practices, including hand hygiene, should be taught and enforced at all healthcare settings.&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25074.s14" sec-type="Pearls and Other Issues">
        <title>Pearls and Other Issues</title>
        <p>Prevention and control of MRSA infections include essential infection-control measures, eg, strict hand hygiene and adequate contact precautions. Hand hygiene means washing hands with soap and water or an alcohol-based cleanser before and after contact with patients with MRSA. Contact precautions include the use of gowns, gloves, and possibly masks during clinical encounters with patients with MRSA infection. Infection control may also include keeping patients in isolated rooms or in the same rooms as other patients with MRSA.</p>
        <p>Other measures include active surveillance to screen for MRSA colonization by testing for PCR MRSA or cultures of nares, oropharynx, or perineum. If found to be positive, contact precautions can be instituted for these patients to prevent transmission of MRSA, as colonization is considered a significant risk factor for MRSA infection. Additional factors that should be kept in mind include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>MRSA infections can involve virtually every organ.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>MRSA has evolved many pathogenic mechanisms that can evade host immunity, invade tissues, produce toxins, and promote biofilm formation.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>MRSA biofilms are intrinsically resistant to antibiotics.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>MRSA is easily transmitted through close skin contact, the sharing of personal hygiene items, and fomites on contaminated surfaces.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Colonization of the nares and oropharynx is a risk factor for infection.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Decolonization strategies using topical mupirocin in combination with chlorhexidine gluconate or dilute bleach baths are frequently effective, but often temporarily successful.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Guidelines for the management and treatment of MRSA infections often lack statistical strength, and therapeutic options should be approached on an individualized&#x000a0;basis.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>MRSA bacteremia is associated with mortality ranging from 20% to 30%.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Published guidelines on the treatment of MRSA bacteremia have not resulted in a significant decline in mortality.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>While the standard of care recommends either vancomycin or daptomycin monotherapy for MRSA bacteremia, combination glycopeptide plus beta-lactam therapy may offer improved outcomes.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Optimal combination therapies will necessitate identifying patients who are most likely to benefit, as well as determining the safest and most effective combinations.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Patients who are intolerant of or failing standard-of-care antibiotic therapy should be considered for salvage therapy with combination antibiotics selected based on the bacteria's specific resistance characteristics and the host's specific clinical features.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Infectious disease consultation should be sought in cases of complicated MRSA infections.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25074.s15" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>MRSA remains a major public health challenge, reflecting decades of bacterial adaptation and shifting epidemiology. Since its identification in 1961, MRSA has evolved from a predominantly hospital-acquired pathogen to one that also thrives in community settings. Despite progress in infection control, the persistence of MRSA colonization and transmission underscores the ongoing need for vigilance, prevention, and evidence-based management strategies to reduce morbidity and mortality.</p>
        <p>Effective MRSA management relies on a coordinated,&#x000a0;interprofessional approach. Physicians, advanced practitioners, and nurses must recognize infection patterns, implement screening protocols, and ensure timely isolation and treatment. Pharmacists play a key role in antibiotic stewardship by optimizing therapy based on culture results and minimizing the development of resistance. Infection control specialists guide adherence to hygiene standards and surveillance. Open communication, shared decision-making, and consistent education among healthcare professionals strengthen patient-centered care, improve safety, and sustain progress in controlling MRSA transmission within healthcare and community environments.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25074.s16">
        <title>Review Questions</title>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>
